<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Free State–Basotho Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1868">1868</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Free State–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> Wars were a series of wars between the Orange Free State and the Basotho kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe I</a>, fought in 1858 and from 1865 to 1868 over the fertile lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caledon-river">Caledon River</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> took much Basotho farmland, and Moshoeshoe sought British protection; Basutoland became a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1868, the origin of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesotho">Lesotho</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/free-state-basotho-wars.jpg" alt="Free State–Basotho Wars" width="320" height="366" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bensusan Museum anon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Moshoeshoe_of_the_Basotho_with_his_ministers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>From 1818 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe I</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> nation out of clans scattered by the wars and famine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mfecane">Mfecane</a>, known in Sesotho as the Difaqane, and in 1824 retreated to the mountain fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu">Thaba Bosiu</a>. Boers crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> settled on land that Moshoeshoe held he had only lent them. A treaty of 1845 recognised Boer settlement but drew no boundary, and the Warden line later proclaimed by the British, after Major Henry Douglas Warden, caused deep resentment, since the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caledon-river">Caledon River</a> valley was vital farmland for both sides.</p><p>Moshoeshoe defeated the British at Viervoet in 1851, and in 1852 Sir George Cathcart's troops suffered heavy losses to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> cavalry on the Berea Plateau. In 1854 the British handed the territory to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sand-river-convention">Sand River Convention</a>, and the Orange Free State claimed land the Basotho still regarded as theirs and used for grazing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>President Jacobus Nicolaas Boshoff declared war on 19 March 1858, a date also reported as 22 March. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> could not take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu">Thaba Bosiu</a> and lost heavily, and they destroyed many stations of the Paris Evangelical Society, whose missionaries they blamed for teaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> pride. With his forces dissolving, Boshoff sought a truce, and an armistice was signed on 1 June.</p><p>Boshoff's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brand">Johannes Brand</a> favoured using the Free State's military superiority. In 1865 he opened the Seqiti War, named after the sound of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> cannon, under General Johan Fick. The Free State army seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, destroyed crops and twice tried to storm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu">Thaba Bosiu</a>, and with food exhausted Moshoeshoe accepted the peace of Thaba Bosiu on 11 April 1866. After the killing of two white settlers at Ladybrand in June 1867, a third war began in July 1867, and the Boers overran all of Moshoeshoe's land except Thaba Bosiu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Moshoeshoe turned to Sir Philip Wodehouse, by then Governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, who was instructed to negotiate for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> to become British subjects, and the Free State had to stop the war rather than quarrel with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. In February 1869 the Convention of Aliwal-North fixed the boundaries of Basutoland and gave the Conquered Territory to the Orange Free State. No further fighting followed. Moshoeshoe died in 1870 and was buried on the summit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu">Thaba Bosiu</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Free State–Basotho Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
